TITLE
Compositionality Analysis

CHAPTER
02.cad

ABSTRACT

Component-based design is important for design of complex embedded software. By properly defining the component interfaces, we can form a new component from the composition of some components. The newly formed component may be composed with other components in turn. Thus, the compositionality analysis based on the component interfaces is necessary.
<p>
Ptolemy II has a component architecture based on actor-oriented design. The components are actors with ports, and each actor implements a specific model of computation (MOC). The component interface in Ptolemy II is port (with associated receivers), which abstracts away the function details inside actors but only focuses on the <i>communication dependencies</i> between actors. This complicates the compositionality of components. 
<p>
An example is the hierarchically nested continuous or discrete models. See figure 1.  In both the continuous and discrete models, a feedback loop can not be zero-delay. There must be at least one component in the loop having its input-output relation strictly causal. However, the current component interface does not include causality. We reveal the causality by constructing an input-output dependence attribute for each component and associating it with the component interface (port). The causality is composable and can be propagated across levels of hierarchy. We call the causality one of the <i>functional dependencies</i>, complementary to the communicational dependencies. Thus, at any level of the hierarchy, the scheduler will be able to construct valid schedule with both the dependencies. 
<p>
<img src="CompositionalityAnalysis_1.GIF" alt="Causality hidden in Composite Actor">
<p>
Another difficulty is that the MOC of the composite may not be any of the individual components, even if the MOC's of the individual components are the same. An example is shown in figure 2. In one iteration, the composite actor, firstly reacts to the second input and generates some output, and then, reacts to the first input. It is not the discrete events semantics any more. We have to introduce something similar to the synchronous reactive semantics, which we call Timed SR. The semantics of Timed SR has two folds: one is about the event handling and causality analysis like DE, while the other one is about the fix point at each instance of events like SR. The preliminary research showed that the process of finding optimal schedule of SR models can be improved by introducing the functional dependencies. 
<p>
<img src="CompositionalityAnalysis_2.GIF" alt="Multiple Runs of Composite Actor">
<p>
An even more challenging problem is about the compositionality of distributed components. Normal SR models suffer from the asynchrony of distributed systems, while DE models are powerful on communication networks. We think Timed SR may provide a better semantics to study the composite of distributed systems.
<p>
Reference:
<p>
1. Edward A. Lee, "Embedded Software," Advances in Computers (M. Zelkowitz, editor), Vol. 56, Academic Press, London, 2002.
<p>
2. Stephen A. Edwards and Edward A. Lee,"The Semantics and Execution of a Synchronous Block-Diagram Language," Science of Computer Programming, Vol. 48, no. 1, July 2003. 
<p>
3. A. Benveniste, B. Caillaud, P. Le Guernic, Compositionality in dataLow synchronous languages: specification & distributed code generation, Inform. Comput. 163 (2) (2000) 125-171.

